<header><p class="st-kicker">nomadic confederation · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Southern Xiongnu</h1><p class="st-meta">48 CE – 216 CE · capital Meiji</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Xiongnu who submitted to the Han in 48 CE and settled inside China's northern frontier, where their descendants later founded the first of the Sixteen Kingdoms.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 48 CE Bi, a grandson of the shanyu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huhanye">Huhanye</a>, led eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> tribes to submit to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a> and took the title of shanyu, splitting the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire</a>. In 50 the Han settled his people in the frontier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordos-plateau">Ordos</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, with the shanyu's court at Meiji.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Southern Xiongnu kept their own shanyus and aristocratic lineages but were supervised by a Han official, the Protector of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, and received annual gifts of grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>. Their cavalry fought for the Han against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-xiongnu">Northern Xiongnu</a> and later in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-turban-rebellion">Yellow Turban</a> wars. Over time they took up farming and Chinese ways.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 216 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> detained the shanyu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huchuquan">Huchuquan</a> at Ye and divided the Southern Xiongnu into Five Divisions under Chinese supervision, ending the shanyu's independent authority. In 304 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yuan">Liu Yuan</a>, a descendant of the shanyus, founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/han-zhao">Han Zhao</a>, the first of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="nomadic confederation, 48 CE – 216 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M18 74 V58 Q50 30 82 58 V74 Z"/><path d="M42 74 V60 H58 V74"/><path d="M30 50 L50 36 L70 50"/><path d="M50 36 V26"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">nomadic confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:60.66%;width:3.34%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>48 CE – 216 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>nomadic confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Meiji</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bi, Huchuquan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Southern Shanyu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=132&amp;focus=southern-xiongnu" aria-label="Southern Xiongnu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7124 497 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Southern Xiongnu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7444 753L7444 753M7444 753L7769 1191"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7444 753h0M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7444 753h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7444 753h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=132&amp;focus=southern-xiongnu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
